  "name": "24 HR URINARY POTASSIUM",
  "code": "1011",
  "cost": 0,
  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"150\" data-end=\"711\">The <strong data-start=\"154\" data-end=\"188\">24-hour urinary potassium test</strong> measures the total amount of potassium excreted in the urine over a 24-hour period. Potassium is a crucial electrolyte involved in <strong data-start=\"320\" data-end=\"395\">nerve function, muscle contraction, and maintaining normal heart rhythm</strong>. This test helps evaluate <strong data-start=\"422\" data-end=\"443\">potassium balance</strong> and is particularly useful in diagnosing the cause of <strong data-start=\"498\" data-end=\"535\">hypokalemia (low blood potassium)</strong> or <strong data-start=\"539\" data-end=\"578\">hyperkalemia (high blood potassium)</strong>. It can help determine whether the kidneys are appropriately conserving or excreting potassium in response to abnormal blood levels.</p>\n<p data-start=\"765\" data-end=\"863\">High urinary potassium excretion (especially in the setting of low blood potassium) may suggest:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"864\" data-end=\"1302\">\n<li data-start=\"864\" data-end=\"1078\">\n<p data-start=\"866\" data-end=\"901\"><strong data-start=\"866\" data-end=\"893\">Renal potassium wasting</strong> due to:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"904\" data-end=\"1078\">\n<li data-start=\"904\" data-end=\"957\">\n<p data-start=\"906\" data-end=\"957\"><strong data-start=\"906\" data-end=\"922\">Diuretic use</strong> (e.g., loop or thiazide diuretics)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"960\" data-end=\"1008\">\n<p data-start=\"962\" data-end=\"1008\"><strong data-start=\"962\" data-end=\"984\">Hyperaldosteronism</strong> (e.g., Conn&rsquo;s syndrome)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1011\" data-end=\"1039\">\n<p data-start=\"1013\" data-end=\"1039\"><strong data-start=\"1013\" data-end=\"1039\">Renal tubular acidosis</strong></p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1042\" data-end=\"1078\">\n<p data-start=\"1044\" data-end=\"1078\"><strong data-start=\"1044\" data-end=\"1076\">Bartter or Gitelman syndrome</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1079\" data-end=\"1146\">\n<p data-start=\"1081\" data-end=\"1146\"><strong data-start=\"1081\" data-end=\"1111\">Excessive potassium intake</strong>, either from diet or supplements</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1147\" data-end=\"1230\">\n<p data-start=\"1149\" data-end=\"1230\"><strong data-start=\"1149\" data-end=\"1186\">Recovery from acute kidney injury</strong>, where excretion may temporarily increase</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1231\" data-end=\"1302\">\n<p data-start=\"1233\" data-end=\"1302\"><strong data-start=\"1233\" data-end=\"1257\">Magnesium deficiency</strong>, which can lead to renal potassium wasting</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1304\" data-end=\"1427\">Elevated levels indicate that <strong data-start=\"1334\" data-end=\"1388\">kidneys are losing more potassium than they should</strong>, especially if serum potassium is low.</p>\n<p data-start=\"1304\" data-end=\"1427\">&nbsp;</p>\n<p data-start=\"1478\" data-end=\"1574\">Low urinary potassium levels (especially in the setting of high blood potassium) may indicate:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"1575\" data-end=\"1959\">\n<li data-start=\"1575\" data-end=\"1694\">\n<p data-start=\"1577\" data-end=\"1694\"><strong data-start=\"1577\" data-end=\"1605\">Impaired kidney function</strong> or <strong data-start=\"1609\" data-end=\"1641\">acute/chronic kidney disease</strong>, where the kidneys cannot excrete enough potassium</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1695\" data-end=\"1775\">\n<p data-start=\"1697\" data-end=\"1775\"><strong data-start=\"1697\" data-end=\"1718\">Hypoaldosteronism</strong> or <strong data-start=\"1722\" data-end=\"1747\">adrenal insufficiency</strong> (e.g., Addison&rsquo;s disease)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1776\" data-end=\"1848\">\n<p data-start=\"1778\" data-end=\"1848\"><strong data-start=\"1778\" data-end=\"1812\">Potassium-sparing diuretic use</strong> (e.g., spironolactone, amiloride)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1849\" data-end=\"1922\">\n<p data-start=\"1851\" data-end=\"1922\"><strong data-start=\"1851\" data-end=\"1866\">Dehydration</strong>, where the body retains potassium to maintain balance</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1923\" data-end=\"1959\">\n<p data-start=\"1925\" data-end=\"1959\"><strong data-start=\"1925\" data-end=\"1957\">Low dietary potassium intake</strong></p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1961\" data-end=\"2121\">Low urinary potassium in the presence of high serum potassium may signal a <strong data-start=\"2036\" data-end=\"2090\">failure of the kidneys to excrete excess potassium</strong>, requiring medical evaluation.</p>",
